-- Q8604: Tax-Exempt Organizations [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Not subject to UBIT because the two-week period lacks commercial frequency","Subject to UBIT because any advertising revenue constitutes a trade or business","Not subject to UBIT because total advertising revenue is under the $50,000 threshold","Subject to UBIT because the advertising activity is repeated on an annual basis"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 512 and Treas. Reg. Section 1.513-1(c), an activity is ''regularly carried on'' only if its frequency and continuity are comparable to commercial enterprises. A two-week annual solicitation does not meet this standard when comparable businesses operate year-round.
Wrong (B): Not all advertising revenue constitutes UBIT; the activity must also be regularly carried on and not substantially related to the exempt purpose.
Wrong (C): There is no $50,000 revenue threshold that exempts advertising income from the UBIT analysis.
Wrong (D): Annual recurrence alone does not make an activity ''regularly carried on''; the duration and frequency within each period are the relevant factors.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 8604;

-- Q8734: Tax-Exempt Organizations [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Public charity, because it receives government grants as part of its support","Public charity, because its total receipts exceed the $1,000,000 threshold","Private foundation, because public support is less than one-third of total support","Private foundation, because it derives a significant portion from investment income"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under Section 509(a)(1), a 501(c)(3) is classified as a private foundation unless it meets the one-third public support test. Public and government support totals $250,000 ($100,000 + $150,000), which is only 25% of total $1,000,000 support—below the one-third threshold.
Wrong (A): Receiving government grants alone does not confer public charity status; the grants must constitute at least one-third of total support.
Wrong (B): Total receipt size is irrelevant to the public charity classification; what matters is the proportion of public support.
Wrong (D): Receiving investment income does not automatically make an organization a private foundation; the classification depends on failing the public support test.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 8734;

